  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis reveals a profoundly significant perturbation of **Tyrosine Metabolism (KEGG map00350)** in the treatment group (FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment = 427×). Six of the eleven differentially-abundant metabolites map directly to this pathway: L-Tyrosine (C00082), Dopamine (C03758), Tyramine (C00483), Homogentisic acid (C00544), Dihydrobiopterin (C02953), and Iron (C14819). The extremely high fold-enrichment far exceeds the typical threshold for biological relevance and indicates a robust, coordinated shift in this metabolic module.\n\n**L-Tyrosine** is the central driver. KEGG pathfinding confirms it is immediately upstream of Tyramine (path length = 1 hop) and upstream of Dopamine via a two-reaction sequence. This positions L-Tyrosine as the metabolic origin point for the observed catecholamine perturbations. Dopamine itself is confirmed as a member of tyrosine metabolism and additionally annotates multiple Reactome neurotransmitter pathways (Neurotransmitter release cycle, clearance, and synaptic transmission), tying the peripheral metabolic signal to potential CNS-relevant biology.\n\n**Iron (C14819)** is a required cofactor for dopamine β-hydroxylase, the enzyme that converts Dopamine to Norepinephrine. Its presence in the hit-list suggests possible modulation of this conversion step under the treatment condition.\n\nLosartan (C07072), while not itself a tyrosine metabolite, is a well-known angiotensin II receptor antagonist that can influence catecholamine dynamics and oxidative stress pathways. Its co-occurrence with a strongly perturbed tyrosine axis may indicate indirect effects on neurotransmitter precursor pools.\n\nIn summary, the treatment drives a coordinated alteration in **tyrosine metabolism**, with L-Tyrosine as the primary upstream driver, propagates through to Dopamine and Tyramine, and involves the iron-dependent dopamine β-hydroxylase step. The breadth of pathway coverage (six compounds) and the near-zero FDR make this the dominant biological story for the study.",
